    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Reverse a singly linked list. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "This is a useful interview question because it exposes the candidate's ability to reason about linked lists in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Amazon SDE1; Microsoft SDE; Samsung Bangalore; other reports. Evidence: Amazon explicitly reported \"Reverse a linked list\"; Microsoft reported the same problem; Samsung reported reverse a linked list. Sources: - LeetCode Microsoft SDE interview experience - LeetCode Amazon SDE1 onsite experience - GeeksforGeeks Samsung Bangalore internship experience.",
    sampleAnswer: "Keep three pointers: prev, current, and next. Save current.next before changing the link, point current.next to prev, then move both pointers forward. Every node is visited once, so the time is O(n) and auxiliary space is O(1). The empty-list and one-node cases fall out naturally from the loop.\n\nA concise implementation is shown in the detailed answer.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nKeep three pointers: `prev`, `current`, and `next`. Save `current.next` before changing the link, point `current.next` to `prev`, then move both pointers forward. Every node is visited once, so the time complexity is O(n) and the auxiliary space is O(1). The empty-list and one-node cases fall out naturally from the loop.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef reverse(head):\n    prev = None\n    cur = head\n    while cur:\n        nxt = cur.next\n        cur.next = prev\n        prev, cur = cur, nxt\n    return prev\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose the input is `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> null`. After the algorithm, the links should read `3 -> 2 -> 1 -> null`. The key check is that no node is lost while the links are changed.\n\n**Why this works:**\n\nAt each iteration, `prev` represents the already-reversed portion of the list, while `cur` represents the portion that still needs to be processed. Saving `cur.next` before reversing the pointer ensures that the remaining nodes are not lost.\n\n**Edge cases:**\n\nAn empty list returns `None`, and a one-node list is returned unchanged. The same loop handles both cases naturally.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nThe time complexity is O(n) because every node is visited exactly once. The auxiliary space is O(1) because only a constant number of pointers are used.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Reverse the list iteratively using `prev`, `cur`, and `next`. Save the next node, reverse the current pointer, and advance the pointers. The solution takes O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.", 
