    explanation: "The value of this question is that a good response has to show more than a final result; it should show how the candidate reason about linked lists in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Amazon SDE1; GeeksforGeeks SDE interview. Source: - GeeksforGeeks Amazon SDE-1 interview experience - GeeksforGeeks SDE Android Team interview experience.",
    sampleAnswer: "Walk through the list one block at a time. Before reversing a block, make sure it contains the required number of nodes; then reverse those links and reconnect the previous block. A useful interview detail is to ask what should happen to a final block smaller than k. A common version leaves that block unchanged. The iterative solution is O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nTo reverse a linked list in groups of size `k`, process the list one group at a time. For each group, first check whether at least `k` nodes remain. If so, reverse exactly those `k` nodes, connect the reversed group to the previous part of the list, and continue with the next group. If fewer than `k` nodes remain, leave that final group unchanged unless the problem explicitly says otherwise.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef reverse_k_group(head, k):\n    if head is None or k <= 1:\n        return head\n\n    dummy = ListNode(0)\n    dummy.next = head\n    group_prev = dummy\n\n    while True:\n        # Find the kth node of the current group\n        kth = group_prev\n        for _ in range(k):\n            kth = kth.next\n            if kth is None:\n                return dummy.next\n\n        group_next = kth.next\n\n        # Reverse the current group\n        prev = group_next\n        cur = group_prev.next\n\n        while cur != group_next:\n            nxt = cur.next\n            cur.next = prev\n            prev = cur\n            cur = nxt\n\n        # Connect the previous group to the reversed group\n        old_group_start = group_prev.next\n        group_prev.next = kth\n        group_prev = old_group_start\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 -> 6 -> null` and `k = 2`, the result is `2 -> 1 -> 4 -> 3 -> 6 -> 5 -> null`.\n\nFor `k = 3`, the result is `3 -> 2 -> 1 -> 6 -> 5 -> 4 -> null`.\n\nIf the input is `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5` and `k = 3`, the result is `3 -> 2 -> 1 -> 4 -> 5`, because the final group contains fewer than `k` nodes.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nThe time complexity is O(n), because every node is visited a constant number of times. The auxiliary space is O(1) for the iterative implementation.\n\n**Common mistakes:**\n\nThe most common mistakes are reversing a partial final group when it should remain unchanged, losing the node after the current group, and incorrectly reconnecting the reversed group to the previous or next group.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Process the list in groups of `k`, verify that a complete group exists, reverse exactly those nodes, reconnect the group, and continue. The iterative solution takes O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.",
