    sampleAnswer: "Walk through the list one block at a time. Before reversing a block, make sure it contains the required number of nodes; then reverse those links and reconnect the previous block. A useful interview detail is to ask what should happen to a final block smaller than k. A common version leaves that block unchanged. The iterative solution is O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThis is a specific case of reversing a linked list in groups of `k`, where `k = 3`. Traverse the list three nodes at a time. If three nodes are available, reverse their links and reconnect the reversed group to the previous and next portions of the list. If fewer than three nodes remain, leave them unchanged unless the problem explicitly requires partial reversal.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef reverse_in_threes(head):\n    dummy = ListNode(0)\n    dummy.next = head\n    group_prev = dummy\n\n    while True:\n        first = group_prev.next\n        if first is None:\n            break\n\n        second = first.next\n        if second is None:\n            break\n\n        third = second.next\n        if third is None:\n            break\n\n        after = third.next\n\n        # Reverse three nodes\n        third.next = second\n        second.next = first\n        first.next = after\n\n        # Move to the end of the reversed group\n        group_prev.next = third\n        group_prev = first\n\n    return dummy.next\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 -> 6 -> 7`, the result is:\n\n`3 -> 2 -> 1 -> 6 -> 5 -> 4 -> 7`\n\nThe first three nodes are reversed, then the next three are reversed, while the final single node remains unchanged.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nTime complexity is O(n), and auxiliary space is O(1).\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Treat every three nodes as one group, reverse their pointers, reconnect the group, and continue. If fewer than three nodes remain, leave them unchanged. The algorithm is O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.",
