    explanation: "This question works well in an interview because it forces the candidate to explain reason about linked lists in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Amazon SDE1 onsite interview. Source: LeetCode Amazon SDE1 Seattle interview experience.",
    sampleAnswer: "Use a dummy node so the case where L is the head is handled cleanly. Move to the node just before L, then repeatedly take the next node from the segment and move it to the front of that segment. Only the requested portion changes. The scan is O(n) and the extra space is O(1).",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nTo reverse a linked-list segment between positions `L` and `R`, use a dummy node before the head, move a pointer to the node immediately before position `L`, and then reverse the required segment in place. The rest of the list remains unchanged.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef reverse_between(head, left, right):\n    if head is None or left == right:\n        return head\n\n    dummy = ListNode(0)\n    dummy.next = head\n    prev = dummy\n\n    # Move prev to the node before left\n    for _ in range(left - 1):\n        prev = prev.next\n\n    # Reverse the segment using head insertion\n    cur = prev.next\n\n    for _ in range(right - left):\n        node = cur.next\n        cur.next = node.next\n        node.next = prev.next\n        prev.next = node\n\n    return dummy.next\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5` with `L = 2` and `R = 4`, the segment `2 -> 3 -> 4` is reversed.\n\nThe result is:\n\n`1 -> 4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 5`\n\nThe important part is preserving the connections outside the reversed segment.\n\n**Edge cases:**\n\nIf `L == R`, nothing needs to be changed. If `L == 1`, the head itself changes, which is why using a dummy node simplifies the implementation.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nTime complexity is O(n) in the worst case, while auxiliary space is O(1).\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Locate the node immediately before `L`, reverse only the links between `L` and `R`, and reconnect the segment. A dummy node makes the head-reversal case much easier to handle.",
