    difficulty: Difficulty.MEDIUM,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Determine whether a linked list containing characters is a palindrome. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "The important signal here is not just whether the candidate knows the pattern, but whether they can reason about linked lists in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Amazon interview experience. Source: GeeksforGeeks Amazon Interview Experience Set 250.",
    sampleAnswer: "For a linked list, find the middle with slow and fast pointers, reverse the second half, and compare the two halves node by node. Comparing values-not node addresses-is the important part. This gives O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space; restoring the second half afterward is a good production-minded touch.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA linked list is a palindrome if it reads the same from left to right and right to left. An efficient O(1)-auxiliary-space solution uses the slow-and-fast pointer technique to find the middle, reverses the second half, and then compares the first and second halves node by node.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef is_palindrome(head):\n    if head is None or head.next is None:\n        return True\n\n    slow = fast = head\n\n    # Find the middle\n    while fast and fast.next:\n        slow = slow.next\n        fast = fast.next.next\n\n    # Skip the middle node for an odd-length list\n    if fast:\n        slow = slow.next\n\n    # Reverse the second half\n    second = reverse(slow)\n    first = head\n\n    # Compare both halves\n    while second:\n        if first.val != second.val:\n            return False\n        first = first.next\n        second = second.next\n\n    return True\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor `r -> a -> c -> e -> c -> a -> r`, the list is a palindrome because it reads `racecar` in both directions.\n\nFor `a -> b -> c`, it is not a palindrome.\n\n**Alternative approach:**\n\nA simpler approach is to copy all characters into an array and compare the array with its reverse. That uses O(n) extra space but is easier to implement.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nThe in-place approach takes O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space, ignoring the recursion stack if the helper is implemented recursively. An iterative reverse helper gives strict O(1) auxiliary space.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Find the midpoint with slow and fast pointers, reverse the second half, and compare it with the first half. This gives O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.",
