    explanation: "This is a useful interview question because it exposes the candidate's ability to reason about LRU/cache in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Amazon, Microsoft, Joveo, OnlineSales AI, Flipkart, OLX Groups and other interview experiences. Sources: - LeetCode Amazon SDE1 onsite - LeetCode Microsoft SDE - LeetCode Joveo SDE1 - LeetCode OnlineSales AI SDE2 - GeeksforGeeks Flipkart SDE II - GeeksforGeeks OLX Groups Interview variants reported by Joveo SDE1, Microsoft SDE, OnlineSales AI SDE2, Flipkart SDE II, OLX Groups and other reports include O(1) get/put, a doubly linked list + hash map, and implementing it on paper.",
    sampleAnswer: "The standard LRU design uses two structures together: a hash map for fast key lookup and a doubly linked list for recency order. The map points to list nodes. On get(), move the node to the front; on put(), update or insert at the front and remove the tail when capacity is exceeded. Lookup, unlink, insert, and eviction are O(1) on average, with O(capacity) space.\n\nA concise implementation is shown in the detailed answer.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nAn LRU, or Least Recently Used, cache removes the item that has gone unused for the longest time when the cache reaches its capacity. To implement `get` and `put` in O(1), the standard design combines a hash map with a doubly linked list.\n\nThe hash map provides O(1) lookup by key, while the doubly linked list maintains the usage order. The most recently used item is placed near the front, and the least recently used item is kept near the back.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\nclass Node:\n    def __init__(self, key=0, value=0):\n        self.key = key\n        self.value = value\n        self.prev = None\n        self.next = None\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache = {}\n\n        self.left = Node()   # Least recently used side\n        self.right = Node()  # Most recently used side\n        self.left.next = self.right\n        self.right.prev = self.left\n\n    def remove(self, node):\n        node.prev.next = node.next\n        node.next.prev = node.prev\n\n    def insert_at_end(self, node):\n        prev = self.right.prev\n        prev.next = node\n        node.prev = prev\n        node.next = self.right\n        self.right.prev = node\n\n    def get(self, key):\n        if key not in self.cache:\n            return -1\n\n        node = self.cache[key]\n        self.remove(node)\n        self.insert_at_end(node)\n        return node.value\n\n    def put(self, key, value):\n        if key in self.cache:\n            self.remove(self.cache[key])\n\n        node = Node(key, value)\n        self.cache[key] = node\n        self.insert_at_end(node)\n\n        if len(self.cache) > self.capacity:\n            lru = self.left.next\n            self.remove(lru)\n            del self.cache[lru.key]\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nWith capacity `2`:\n\n`put(A, 1)` -> cache contains A.\n\n`put(B, 2)` -> cache order is A, B.\n\n`get(A)` -> A becomes most recently used, so order becomes B, A.\n\n`put(C, 3)` -> capacity is exceeded, so B, the least recently used item, is evicted.\n\nThe remaining cache contains A and C.\n\n**Why both data structures are needed:**\n\nA hash map alone gives fast lookup but cannot efficiently identify and remove the least recently used item. A linked list alone can maintain order but searching for a key would be O(n). Combining them gives O(1) average lookup, update, movement, and eviction.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\n- `get`: O(1) average.\n- `put`: O(1) average.\n- Space: O(capacity).\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Use a hash map for O(1) key lookup and a doubly linked list for O(1) movement and eviction. Keep the most recently used node at one end and the least recently used node at the other.",
