    experienceLevel: ExperienceLevel.EXPERIENCED,
    difficulty: Difficulty.HARD,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Modify an LRU cache to support TTL/expiration. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "The value of this question is that a good response has to show more than a final result; it should show how the candidate reason about LRU/cache in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Microsoft Senior Software Engineer. Source: LeetCode Microsoft Senior SE interview experience, March 2026.",
    sampleAnswer: "Keep the normal LRU structure-a hash map plus a doubly linked list-but add an expiration timestamp to each entry. A get() first checks whether the key exists and whether its TTL has expired; an expired entry is removed before returning a miss. Capacity eviction still removes the least-recently-used entry. Average get/put can remain O(1), but proactive cleanup of expired entries may require an additional time-based structure such as a min-heap if expired keys must be removed even when they are never accessed.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nTo add TTL, or time-to-live, to an LRU cache, each cache entry needs an expiration timestamp in addition to its key and value. A `get` operation should check whether the entry has expired before returning it, and expired entries should be removed. `put` calculates the expiration time from the current time and the requested TTL.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\nimport time\n\n\nclass Entry:\n    def __init__(self, key, value, expires_at):\n        self.key = key\n        self.value = value\n        self.expires_at = expires_at\n        self.prev = None\n        self.next = None\n\n\nclass TTLCache:\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache = {}\n\n    def get(self, key):\n        entry = self.cache.get(key)\n\n        if entry is None:\n            return None\n\n        if entry.expires_at <= time.monotonic():\n            self._remove(entry)\n            return None\n\n        self._move_to_mru(entry)\n        return entry.value\n\n    def put(self, key, value, ttl_seconds):\n        expires_at = time.monotonic() + ttl_seconds\n\n        if key in self.cache:\n            self._remove(self.cache[key])\n\n        entry = Entry(key, value, expires_at)\n        self.cache[key] = entry\n        self._add_mru(entry)\n\n        self._evict_if_needed()\n```\n\n**Important design issue:**\n\nTTL and LRU solve different problems. LRU answers **which entry should be evicted when capacity is reached**, while TTL answers **whether an entry is still valid based on time**.\n\nAn entry can therefore be both recently used and expired. Conversely, an entry can be old in terms of usage but still within its TTL.\n\n**Expiration strategy:**\n\nThere are two common approaches:\n\n1. **Lazy expiration:** Check expiry during `get` or other operations. This is simple but expired entries may remain in memory if they are never accessed.\n2. **Active expiration:** Run a background cleanup mechanism to remove expired entries. This prevents stale entries from accumulating but introduces scheduling and concurrency considerations.\n\nFor a production cache, I would also define behavior for updates, zero or negative TTLs, clock handling, cleanup frequency, and what happens when expired entries consume capacity before cleanup occurs.\n\nUsing a monotonic clock for elapsed TTL avoids problems caused by wall-clock adjustments.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Add an expiration timestamp to every LRU entry. Check the timestamp on reads and remove expired entries, while retaining the LRU ordering for capacity-based eviction. For production, decide between lazy and active expiration and handle concurrency carefully.",
