    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Implement LRU cache on paper. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "This question works well in an interview because it forces the candidate to explain reason about LRU/cache in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: OLX Groups. Source: GeeksforGeeks OLX Groups Interview Experience. ==================================================== 3. ARRAYS ====================================================.",
    sampleAnswer: "The standard LRU design uses two structures together: a hash map for fast key lookup and a doubly linked list for recency order. The map points to list nodes. On get(), move the node to the front; on put(), update or insert at the front and remove the tail when capacity is exceeded. Lookup, unlink, insert, and eviction are O(1) on average, with O(capacity) space.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nFor a whiteboard implementation, I would first state the requirement: `get(key)` and `put(key, value)` should both run in O(1), and when the cache exceeds capacity, the least recently used entry must be removed.\n\nThe standard design is:\n\n**Hash map + doubly linked list**\n\nThe hash map stores:\n\n`key -> node`\n\nThe doubly linked list stores entries in usage order.\n\nFor example:\n\n`LRU <-> A <-> B <-> C <-> MRU`\n\nHere, `A` is the least recently used item and `C` is the most recently used item.\n\n**Paper implementation:**\n\n```text\nget(key):\n    if key not in map:\n        return -1\n\n    node = map[key]\n    remove node from its current position\n    move node to MRU position\n    return node.value\n\nput(key, value):\n    if key exists:\n        remove existing node\n\n    create node(key, value)\n    add node at MRU position\n    map[key] = node\n\n    if size > capacity:\n        lru = node immediately after LRU sentinel\n        remove lru\n        delete map[lru.key]\n```\n\n**Why a doubly linked list?**\n\nWhen a node is accessed, it needs to move from its current position to the MRU position. A doubly linked list allows removal in O(1) because the node has direct references to both its previous and next nodes.\n\nA singly linked list would make removing an arbitrary node more expensive because we would need to find its predecessor.\n\n**Example:**\n\nCapacity = 2.\n\n`put(A, 1)`\n\n`A`\n\n`put(B, 2)`\n\n`A <-> B`\n\n`get(A)`\n\n`B <-> A`\n\n`put(C, 3)`\n\n`B` is the least recently used entry, so remove it:\n\n`A <-> C`\n\n**Complexity:**\n\n- `get`: O(1) average.\n- `put`: O(1) average.\n- Eviction: O(1).\n- Space: O(capacity).\n\n**What I would say while coding:**\n\n> I need fast lookup and fast recency updates, so I will combine a hash map with a doubly linked list. The map tells me where a key's node is, and the list maintains LRU-to-MRU order. Every operation changes only a constant number of pointers, giving O(1) average time.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> On paper, start with the requirement for O(1) `get` and `put`, choose a hash map for lookup and a doubly linked list for ordering, define LRU and MRU ends, and then implement lookup, move-to-MRU, insertion, and eviction.",
