    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Solve a permutation-in-string problem. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "The value of this question is that a good response has to show more than a final result; it should show how the candidate reason about strings in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Microsoft SDE2 India. Source: LeetCode Microsoft SDE2 India interview experience.",
    sampleAnswer: "Use a fixed-size frequency window. Build the character counts for the pattern, slide a window of the same length across the text, add the new character, remove the old one, and compare the counts. With a fixed alphabet, the comparison can be treated as O(1), giving O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space for the alphabet counts.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe Permutation in String problem asks whether one string contains a contiguous substring that is a permutation of another string. The key is that permutations have the same character frequencies, so we can use a sliding window with character counts.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef check_inclusion(s1, s2):\n    if len(s1) > len(s2):\n        return False\n\n    need = [0] * 26\n    window = [0] * 26\n\n    for ch in s1:\n        need[ord(ch) - ord('a')] += 1\n\n    for i, ch in enumerate(s2):\n        window[ord(ch) - ord('a')] += 1\n\n        if i >= len(s1):\n            old = s2[i - len(s1)]\n            window[ord(old) - ord('a')] -= 1\n\n        if window == need:\n            return True\n\n    return False\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor `s1 = \"ab\"` and `s2 = \"eidbaooo\"`, the substring `\"ba\"` is a permutation of `\"ab\"`, so the answer is `True`.\n\nThe window always has the same length as `s1`. Instead of generating every permutation, we compare character frequencies.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nFor a fixed lowercase alphabet, the solution is O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space because the frequency arrays contain only 26 entries.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Use a fixed-size sliding window and character-frequency counts. A window is valid when its frequency distribution matches the frequency distribution of the target string.",
