    subcategorySlug: "strings",
    experienceLevel: ExperienceLevel.FRESHER,
    difficulty: Difficulty.HARD,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Solve Next Permutation. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "This question works well in an interview because it forces the candidate to explain reason about strings in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Amazon SDE1. Source: LeetCode interview experience by candidate reporting Amazon SDE1.",
    sampleAnswer: "Scan from the end to find the first index i where a[i] < a[i+1]. Then find the smallest value to the right that is larger than a[i], swap them, and reverse the suffix. If no such i exists, the sequence is already the largest permutation, so simply reverse it to obtain the smallest. The algorithm is O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.\n\nA concise implementation is shown in the detailed answer.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nNext Permutation asks for the lexicographically next greater arrangement of the same elements. The standard in-place algorithm finds the longest non-increasing suffix, identifies the pivot immediately before it, swaps the pivot with the smallest larger element in the suffix, and reverses the suffix.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef next_permutation(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    i = n - 2\n\n    # Find the first decreasing position from the right\n    while i >= 0 and nums[i] >= nums[i + 1]:\n        i -= 1\n\n    if i >= 0:\n        j = n - 1\n        while nums[j] <= nums[i]:\n            j -= 1\n        nums[i], nums[j] = nums[j], nums[i]\n\n    # Reverse the suffix\n    left = i + 1\n    right = n - 1\n    while left < right:\n        nums[left], nums[right] = nums[right], nums[left]\n        left += 1\n        right -= 1\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor `[1, 2, 3]`, the next permutation is `[1, 3, 2]`.\n\nFor `[1, 3, 2]`, it becomes `[2, 1, 3]`.\n\nFor the largest permutation `[3, 2, 1]`, there is no larger permutation, so the algorithm reverses the entire array to produce `[1, 2, 3]`.\n\nThe suffix is reversed because it is already in non-increasing order, and after swapping the pivot, reversing it produces the smallest possible suffix that is still greater than the original permutation.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nTime complexity is O(n), and auxiliary space is O(1).\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Find the rightmost position where the sequence increases, swap that pivot with the smallest larger value to its right, then reverse the suffix. This produces the next lexicographically greater permutation in O(n) time and O(1) space.",
