    subcategorySlug: "strings",
    experienceLevel: ExperienceLevel.EXPERIENCED,
    difficulty: Difficulty.EASY,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Solve Permutation in String. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "The value of this question is that a good response has to show more than a final result; it should show how the candidate reason about strings in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Microsoft SDE2 India. Source: LeetCode Microsoft SDE2 India interview experience.",
    sampleAnswer: "Use a fixed-size frequency window. Build the character counts for the pattern, slide a window of the same length across the text, add the new character, remove the old one, and compare the counts. With a fixed alphabet, the comparison can be treated as O(1), giving O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space for the alphabet counts.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nPermutation in String is solved efficiently with a sliding window. The target string has a fixed character-frequency requirement, so we maintain a window of exactly the same length in the larger string and track its character frequencies.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef check_inclusion(s1, s2):\n    if len(s1) > len(s2):\n        return False\n\n    need = [0] * 26\n    window = [0] * 26\n\n    for ch in s1:\n        need[ord(ch) - ord('a')] += 1\n\n    for i, ch in enumerate(s2):\n        window[ord(ch) - ord('a')] += 1\n\n        if i >= len(s1):\n            window[ord(s2[i - len(s1)]) - ord('a')] -= 1\n\n        if window == need:\n            return True\n\n    return False\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor `s1 = \"ab\"` and `s2 = \"eidbaooo\"`, the window eventually becomes `\"ba\"`. Since `\"ba\"` contains exactly the same character counts as `\"ab\"`, it is a permutation and the function returns `True`.\n\nThe important insight is that we do not need to generate permutations. We only need to determine whether any substring has the same frequency distribution.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nWith a fixed alphabet of 26 lowercase letters, the algorithm runs in O(n) time and uses O(1) auxiliary space.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Maintain a fixed-size sliding window and compare its character frequencies with the target's frequencies. This avoids generating permutations and gives linear time for a fixed alphabet.",
