    difficulty: Difficulty.MEDIUM,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Reverse a stack using recursion. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "An interviewer can learn a lot from this problem by listening for whether the candidate reason about stacks and queues in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: PharmEasy SDE1. Source: LeetCode candidate interview experience.",
    sampleAnswer: "Use recursion to remove the top element until the stack is empty. Then insert each removed element at the bottom while the recursive calls return. The key helper is insertAtBottom, which temporarily removes elements until the bottom is reached. This uses O(n2) time in the straightforward implementation and O(n) call-stack space.\n\nA concise implementation is shown in the detailed answer.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nTo reverse a stack using recursion, remove the top element recursively until the stack becomes empty, then insert each removed element at the bottom while the recursion unwinds. This reverses the order without using an explicit second stack.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef insert_at_bottom(stack, value):\n    if not stack:\n        stack.append(value)\n        return\n\n    top = stack.pop()\n    insert_at_bottom(stack, value)\n    stack.append(top)\n\n\ndef reverse_stack(stack):\n    if not stack:\n        return\n\n    top = stack.pop()\n    reverse_stack(stack)\n    insert_at_bottom(stack, top)\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nSuppose the stack is:\n\n`[1, 2, 3, 4]`\n\nwhere `4` is the top. The recursion removes `4`, then `3`, then `2`, then `1`. When recursion returns, each element is inserted at the bottom, producing the reversed stack:\n\n`[4, 3, 2, 1]`\n\n**Why it works:**\n\nThe recursive calls temporarily hold the removed elements on the call stack. Once the smallest subproblem is reached, the elements are inserted at the bottom in reverse order.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nThe straightforward implementation takes O(n²) time because `insert_at_bottom` can traverse the stack recursively for each element. The recursion uses O(n) call-stack space.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Recursively remove every element, then insert each element at the bottom during recursion unwinding. This reverses the stack without using another explicit data structure, but costs O(n²) time and O(n) recursion space.",
