    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Solve a stack-based coding problem. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "This question works well in an interview because it forces the candidate to explain reason about stacks and queues in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: GeeksforGeeks SDE interview. Source: GeeksforGeeks SDE Android Team interview experience.",
    sampleAnswer: "First identify what the stack is buying you: usually it stores unresolved elements or remembers the most recent state. Common interview patterns include matching parentheses, next greater elements, monotonic stacks, and expression evaluation. State the invariant-for example, a monotonic stack keeps candidates in a defined order-and then analyze how many times each element is pushed and popped.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA common stack-based coding problem is evaluating a postfix expression. In postfix notation, operators appear after their operands, so a stack can store intermediate values. When an operator appears, pop the required operands, perform the operation, and push the result back.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef evaluate_postfix(tokens):\n    stack = []\n\n    for token in tokens:\n        if token not in {\"+\", \"-\", \"*\", \"/\"}:\n            stack.append(int(token))\n            continue\n\n        b = stack.pop()\n        a = stack.pop()\n\n        if token == \"+\":\n            result = a + b\n        elif token == \"-\":\n            result = a - b\n        elif token == \"*\":\n            result = a * b\n        else:\n            result = int(a / b)\n\n        stack.append(result)\n\n    return stack[-1]\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor `2 3 + 4 *`, push `2`, push `3`, apply `+` to get `5`, push `4`, then apply `*` to get `20`.\n\nThe important stack rule is that when an operator is encountered, the second value popped is the left operand and the first value popped is the right operand. This matters for subtraction and division.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nIf there are `n` tokens, time complexity is O(n) and auxiliary space is O(n) in the worst case.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Use a stack to store operands and intermediate results. Push values, and whenever an operator appears, pop the required operands, calculate the result, and push it back.",
