    experienceLevel: ExperienceLevel.FRESHER,
    difficulty: Difficulty.EASY,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Design a stack that supports getMin in O(1). Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "The important signal here is not just whether the candidate knows the pattern, but whether they can reason about stacks and queues in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: OLX Groups. Source: GeeksforGeeks OLX Groups Interview Experience.",
    sampleAnswer: "Use a second stack that stores the minimum value seen at each depth. On push, push min(value, currentMinimum) onto the minimum stack; on pop, remove from both stacks. Then getMin() simply reads the top of the minimum stack. All three operations are O(1), with O(n) extra space.\n\nA concise implementation is shown in the detailed answer.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nTo design a stack that supports `push`, `pop`, `top`, and `getMin` in O(1), maintain two stacks. The normal stack stores all values, while the minimum stack stores the minimum value applicable to each state of the stack.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\nclass MinStack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.stack = []\n        self.min_stack = []\n\n    def push(self, value):\n        self.stack.append(value)\n\n        if not self.min_stack:\n            self.min_stack.append(value)\n        else:\n            self.min_stack.append(\n                min(value, self.min_stack[-1])\n            )\n\n    def pop(self):\n        if not self.stack:\n            raise IndexError(\"pop from empty stack\")\n\n        self.min_stack.pop()\n        return self.stack.pop()\n\n    def top(self):\n        if not self.stack:\n            raise IndexError(\"top from empty stack\")\n        return self.stack[-1]\n\n    def get_min(self):\n        if not self.min_stack:\n            raise IndexError(\"min from empty stack\")\n        return self.min_stack[-1]\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nPush `5`, `3`, and `7`.\n\nThe main stack is `[5, 3, 7]`.\n\nThe minimum stack is `[5, 3, 3]`.\n\nTherefore `get_min()` returns `3` in O(1). If `7` is popped, the minimum remains `3`. If `3` is then popped, the minimum becomes `5`.\n\n**Alternative design:**\n\nWe can store pairs such as `(value, current_min)` in one stack. That also provides O(1) operations.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\n- `push`: O(1)\n- `pop`: O(1)\n- `top`: O(1)\n- `get_min`: O(1)\n- Space: O(n)\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Use a second stack to track the minimum corresponding to each stack state. Every operation, including `getMin`, can then be performed in O(1).",
