    subcategorySlug: "trees",
    experienceLevel: ExperienceLevel.FRESHER,
    difficulty: Difficulty.MEDIUM,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Find the bottom view of a binary tree. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "This problem gives the interviewer a quick way to judge how the candidate reasons about reason about trees in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Amazon. Source: GeeksforGeeks Amazon Interview Experience Set 250.",
    sampleAnswer: "Assign each node a horizontal distance from the root. During a level-order traversal, record the node seen at each horizontal distance, replacing earlier values as deeper nodes are encountered. BFS is convenient because nodes are processed from top to bottom. With a map keyed by horizontal distance, the time is O(n) and space is O(n).",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe bottom view of a binary tree contains the nodes visible when the tree is viewed from below. For each horizontal distance from the root, the bottom-most node should be selected. A common solution uses level-order traversal with a queue while tracking each node's horizontal distance.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\n\ndef bottom_view(root):\n    if root is None:\n        return []\n\n    view = {}\n    queue = deque([(root, 0)])\n\n    while queue:\n        node, hd = queue.popleft()\n\n        # Later nodes at the same horizontal distance\n        # are lower in level-order traversal.\n        view[hd] = node.val\n\n        if node.left:\n            queue.append((node.left, hd - 1))\n        if node.right:\n            queue.append((node.right, hd + 1))\n\n    return [view[hd] for hd in sorted(view)]\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor a tree where multiple nodes share the same horizontal distance, the node appearing at the greatest depth should be selected for the bottom view. With level-order traversal, overwriting the value for each horizontal distance naturally retains the latest node encountered.\n\nThe exact result can depend on the tie-breaking rule when multiple nodes have the same depth and horizontal distance, so I would clarify that rule if the interviewer specifies one.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nTime complexity is O(n log n) with a dictionary followed by sorting horizontal distances. With an ordered map, similar complexity applies. Auxiliary space is O(n).\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Perform BFS while tracking each node's horizontal distance from the root. For every horizontal distance, overwrite the stored value so the bottom-most node remains. Finally output the values from leftmost to rightmost horizontal distance.",
