    explanation: "This is a useful interview question because it exposes the candidate's ability to reason about trees in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Amazon SDE1. Source: GeeksforGeeks Amazon Interview Experience SDE-1.",
    sampleAnswer: "Use horizontal distance from the root and a BFS. The first node encountered at a horizontal distance is the node visible from the top, so store that value only if the distance has not been seen before. BFS naturally gives the required top-to-bottom order. Time is O(n) and extra space is O(n).",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe top view of a binary tree contains the nodes visible when the tree is viewed from above. For each horizontal distance from the root, we keep the first node encountered during level-order traversal because BFS encounters shallower nodes before deeper nodes.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\n\ndef top_view(root):\n    if root is None:\n        return []\n\n    view = {}\n    queue = deque([(root, 0)])\n\n    while queue:\n        node, hd = queue.popleft()\n\n        if hd not in view:\n            view[hd] = node.val\n\n        if node.left:\n            queue.append((node.left, hd - 1))\n        if node.right:\n            queue.append((node.right, hd + 1))\n\n    return [view[hd] for hd in sorted(view)]\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nAssign horizontal distance `0` to the root. A left child gets `-1`, a right child gets `+1`, and the distance changes by one as we move left or right. During BFS, the first node encountered at each horizontal distance is the node selected for the top view.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nTime complexity is O(n log n) if the horizontal distances are sorted at the end. Auxiliary space is O(n).\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Use BFS with horizontal-distance tracking and store only the first node encountered at each horizontal distance. Sorting the distances gives the top view from left to right.",
