    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Find nodes at distance K from a given binary-tree node. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "The important signal here is not just whether the candidate knows the pattern, but whether they can reason about trees in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Flipkart SDE II. Source: GeeksforGeeks Flipkart Interview Set 7.",
    sampleAnswer: "A binary tree is naturally directed downward, but distance-K can require moving from a node to its parent. One clean approach is to build a parent map first, then run BFS from the target treating left, right, and parent as neighbors. Stop after K levels. The traversal is O(n) time and O(n) space in the worst case.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nTo find all nodes at distance `K` from a target node in a binary tree, we must be able to move both downward to children and upward to the parent. Since ordinary binary-tree nodes do not contain parent pointers, a common solution first builds a parent map, then performs BFS starting from the target node.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\n\ndef distance_k(root, target, k):\n    parent = {}\n    queue = deque([root])\n\n    while queue:\n        node = queue.popleft()\n\n        if node.left:\n            parent[node.left] = node\n            queue.append(node.left)\n\n        if node.right:\n            parent[node.right] = node\n            queue.append(node.right)\n\n    queue = deque([target])\n    visited = {target}\n    distance = 0\n\n    while queue:\n        if distance == k:\n            return [node.val for node in queue]\n\n        for _ in range(len(queue)):\n            node = queue.popleft()\n\n            neighbors = [node.left, node.right, parent.get(node)]\n\n            for neighbor in neighbors:\n                if neighbor and neighbor not in visited:\n                    visited.add(neighbor)\n                    queue.append(neighbor)\n\n        distance += 1\n\n    return []\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nConsider a tree with target node `5`. At distance `1`, its neighbors are its children and its parent. At distance `2`, we continue BFS through those neighbors while preventing already visited nodes from being processed again.\n\nThe parent map effectively converts the tree into an undirected graph, allowing movement in all three directions: left child, right child, and parent.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nBuilding the parent map takes O(n) time and O(n) space. The BFS also takes O(n) time in the worst case and O(n) space.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Build parent pointers, then perform BFS from the target node while treating each node's left child, right child, and parent as neighbors. Stop when BFS reaches distance `K`.",
