    sampleAnswer: "At every node, swap its left and right children and then do the same recursively or iteratively for the children. The important detail is that you swap first, then continue with the resulting children. Every node is visited once, so the time is O(n) and recursion space is O(h).",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nTo mirror a binary tree, swap the left and right child of every node recursively or iteratively. The operation changes the structure so that the tree becomes its reflection across a vertical axis.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef mirror(root):\n    if root is None:\n        return None\n\n    root.left, root.right = root.right, root.left\n\n    mirror(root.left)\n    mirror(root.right)\n\n    return root\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nIf the original tree is:\n\n`    1`\n\n`   / \\\\`\n\n`  2   3`\n\n` / \\\\`\n\n`4   5`\n\nAfter mirroring it becomes:\n\n`    1`\n\n`   / \\\\`\n\n`  3   2`\n\n`     / \\\\`\n\n`    5   4`\n\nEvery node has its left and right children exchanged.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nTime complexity is O(n), because every node is visited once. Recursive auxiliary space is O(h), where `h` is the tree height, and O(n) in the worst case for a skewed tree.\n\nAn iterative BFS or DFS implementation can also perform the swaps and uses O(n) or O(h) auxiliary space depending on the traversal structure.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Traverse every node and swap its left and right children. This mirrors the tree in O(n) time.",
