    experienceLevel: ExperienceLevel.FRESHER,
    difficulty: Difficulty.HARD,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Solve a backtracking problem based on N-Queens. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "The value of this question is that a good response has to show more than a final result; it should show how the candidate reason about graphs/backtracking in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: PharmEasy SDE1. Source: LeetCode candidate interview experience.",
    sampleAnswer: "Place one queen per row and backtrack when a placement conflicts with an earlier queen. Track occupied columns and the two diagonal families so a candidate position can be checked in O(1). When a row is complete, record the board or count the solution. The search is exponential in the worst case, which is expected for the general N-Queens problem.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nN-Queens is a classic backtracking problem. The goal is to place `N` queens on an `N × N` chessboard so that no two queens attack each other. Because queens attack along rows, columns, and diagonals, we place one queen per row and track which columns and diagonals are already occupied.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef solve_n_queens(n):\n    result = []\n    board = [['.'] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    columns = set()\n    diag1 = set()  # row - col\n    diag2 = set()  # row + col\n\n    def backtrack(row):\n        if row == n:\n            result.append([''.join(r) for r in board])\n            return\n\n        for col in range(n):\n            if col in columns:\n                continue\n            if row - col in diag1:\n                continue\n            if row + col in diag2:\n                continue\n\n            board[row][col] = 'Q'\n            columns.add(col)\n            diag1.add(row - col)\n            diag2.add(row + col)\n\n            backtrack(row + 1)\n\n            board[row][col] = '.'\n            columns.remove(col)\n            diag1.remove(row - col)\n            diag2.remove(row + col)\n\n    backtrack(0)\n    return result\n```\n\n**How backtracking works:**\n\nAt each row, try every column that is currently safe. If placing a queen eventually prevents completion of the board, undo that placement and try another column.\n\nThis follows the pattern:\n\n`choose -> explore -> undo`\n\nThe sets make conflict checking O(1) on average instead of scanning previously placed queens each time.\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor `N = 4`, one valid arrangement is:\n\n`..Q.`\n\n`Q...`\n\n`...Q`\n\n`.Q..`\n\nNo two queens share a column or diagonal.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nThe search is exponential in nature. A commonly cited upper-bound description is O(N!) for the backtracking search when one queen is placed per row and columns are tracked, although the exact practical runtime depends heavily on pruning. Space is O(N²) for storing the board plus O(N) recursion/state structures, excluding the output.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Place one queen per row, reject occupied columns and diagonals, recursively continue, and undo the placement when a branch fails. This is the standard choose-explore-undo backtracking pattern.",
