    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Design Git using a graph-based approach. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "An interviewer can learn a lot from this problem by listening for whether the candidate reason about graphs/backtracking in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Samsung Bangalore internship. Source: GeeksforGeeks Samsung Bangalore SRIB Internship Experience.",
    sampleAnswer: "A graph model fits Git because commits form a directed acyclic graph: each commit points to one or more parent commits. A branch is essentially a movable reference to a commit, while merge commits can have multiple parents. In an interview, focus on the data model and operations-create commit, move branch, traverse history, and find common ancestors-rather than reproducing Git's entire implementation.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nIf asked to design Git using graph concepts, I would model Git's commit history as a directed acyclic graph. Each commit is a node, and a parent relationship is a directed edge from a commit to its parent. Branches and tags are references to particular commit nodes.\n\n**Conceptual model:**\n\n```text\nCommit C\n   |\nCommit B ---- Commit D\n   |             |\nCommit A --------+\n```\n\nA normal commit has one parent, while a merge commit can have multiple parents. Because commits reference previous commits, the history forms a DAG rather than a simple linear list.\n\n**Key Git concepts:**\n\n- **Commit:** Node in the graph.\n- **Parent:** Directed edge to an earlier commit.\n- **Branch:** A movable reference pointing to a commit.\n- **HEAD:** Reference representing the currently checked-out position.\n- **Merge:** Creates a commit with multiple parents when histories are combined.\n- **Reachability:** A commit is an ancestor if it can be reached by repeatedly following parent edges.\n\n**Finding common history:**\n\nOperations such as finding a merge base can be viewed as graph traversal problems. We can traverse ancestors of one commit, then traverse ancestors of another until finding an appropriate common ancestor according to Git's rules.\n\n**Why a DAG?**\n\nA commit points backward to earlier commits, so the history does not normally contain cycles. The graph allows branches to diverge and later merge while preserving the complete history.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Model commits as nodes and parent relationships as directed edges, producing a DAG. Branches are references to nodes, and merges create nodes with multiple parents. Many Git operations reduce to graph traversal and reachability problems.",
