    explanation: "This question works well in an interview because it forces the candidate to explain reason about trees in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Samsung Bangalore internship. Source: GeeksforGeeks Samsung Bangalore SRIB Internship Experience. ==================================================== 8. DYNAMIC PROGRAMMING / GREEDY ====================================================.",
    sampleAnswer: "A tree is a special kind of graph: it is connected and has no cycles. That lets graph ideas such as DFS, BFS, parent tracking, and distance traversal be reused, while the absence of cycles simplifies many algorithms. If you model a tree as an undirected graph, remember to avoid immediately walking back to the parent.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nMany tree problems can be solved using graph concepts because a tree is a connected graph with no cycles. The main difference is that a tree has a natural hierarchy, while a general graph may contain cycles and multiple paths between nodes.\n\nA useful example is finding nodes at distance `K` from a target. In a tree, ordinary child traversal only moves downward, but graph-style traversal lets us treat the parent as another neighbor and use BFS.\n\n**Implementation idea:**\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\n\ndef nodes_at_distance_k(root, target, k):\n    parent = {}\n    queue = deque([root])\n\n    while queue:\n        node = queue.popleft()\n        for child in (node.left, node.right):\n            if child:\n                parent[child] = node\n                queue.append(child)\n\n    queue = deque([target])\n    visited = {target}\n    distance = 0\n\n    while queue:\n        if distance == k:\n            return [node.val for node in queue]\n\n        for _ in range(len(queue)):\n            node = queue.popleft()\n            neighbors = [node.left, node.right, parent.get(node)]\n\n            for neighbor in neighbors:\n                if neighbor and neighbor not in visited:\n                    visited.add(neighbor)\n                    queue.append(neighbor)\n\n        distance += 1\n\n    return []\n```\n\nThe tree becomes an implicit undirected graph where each node can have up to three neighbors: left child, right child, and parent.\n\nOther graph concepts useful for tree problems include BFS, DFS, connected components, shortest paths, topological-style reasoning for DAGs, and visited-state tracking when the representation permits cycles.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> A tree is a special case of a graph, so graph traversal techniques such as BFS and DFS can solve many tree problems. Adding parent relationships can turn hierarchical movement into general graph traversal.",
