    experienceLevel: ExperienceLevel.FRESHER,
    difficulty: Difficulty.EASY,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Find the maximum/minimum-related values in a binary tree. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "This is a useful interview question because it exposes the candidate's ability to reason about trees in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Samsung Bangalore internship. Source: GeeksforGeeks Samsung Bangalore SRIB Internship Experience. ==================================================== 10. REPEATED / HIGH-PRIORITY VERIFIED TOPICS ==================================================== The following have particularly strong evidence because they appear in multiple candidate reports:.",
    sampleAnswer: "Traverse the tree once and update the required extreme value as each node is visited. If the question asks for a second-smallest or second-largest value, keep the required number of candidates rather than sorting every value unless the constraints justify it. For a simple min/max query, the time is O(n) and the auxiliary space is O(h) with recursive DFS.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nTo find the maximum or minimum value in a binary tree, traverse every node and keep track of the best value seen so far. Unlike a Binary Search Tree, a normal binary tree has no ordering guarantee, so we cannot simply go to the rightmost or leftmost node.\n\nA DFS traversal is sufficient. For every node, compare its value with the current maximum or minimum and then recursively process its left and right children.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef find_max(root):\n    if root is None:\n        return float('-inf')\n\n    return max(root.val, find_max(root.left), find_max(root.right))\n\n\ndef find_min(root):\n    if root is None:\n        return float('inf')\n\n    return min(root.val, find_min(root.left), find_min(root.right))\n```\n\nAn iterative DFS or BFS can also be used when avoiding recursion is important.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nEvery node must be examined, so the time complexity is O(n). Recursive DFS uses O(h) auxiliary space, where h is the tree height. In the worst case of a skewed tree, this becomes O(n), while a balanced tree requires O(log n).\n\n**Common mistake:**\n\nDo not assume that the maximum value is on the right or the minimum value is on the left unless the input is explicitly a Binary Search Tree.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Traverse the entire binary tree and maintain the maximum or minimum value encountered. A normal binary tree has no ordering property, so every node may need to be inspected.",
