    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Common reported pattern: Reverse a linked list in groups. Source summary: recurring pattern in the supplied interview evidence bank; not a claim of one exact verbatim interview question.. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "The value of this question is that a good response has to show more than a final result; it should show how the candidate reason about linked lists in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code.",
    sampleAnswer: "Walk through the list one block at a time. Before reversing a block, make sure it contains the required number of nodes; then reverse those links and reconnect the previous block. A useful interview detail is to ask what should happen to a final block smaller than k. A common version leaves that block unchanged. The iterative solution is O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nTo reverse a linked list in groups of size k, process one group at a time. Before reversing a group, first verify that it contains k nodes. Reverse exactly those k nodes, connect the previous group to the reversed group, and then continue with the next group.\n\nIf the remaining nodes are fewer than k, the common requirement is to leave that final group unchanged. This behavior should be confirmed from the problem statement because some variants reverse the final incomplete group as well.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef reverse_k_group(head, k):\n    dummy = ListNode(0)\n    dummy.next = head\n    group_prev = dummy\n\n    while True:\n        kth = group_prev\n        for _ in range(k):\n            kth = kth.next\n            if kth is None:\n                return dummy.next\n\n        group_next = kth.next\n\n        prev = group_next\n        cur = group_prev.next\n\n        while cur != group_next:\n            nxt = cur.next\n            cur.next = prev\n            prev = cur\n            cur = nxt\n\n        old_start = group_prev.next\n        group_prev.next = kth\n        group_prev = old_start\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5 -> 6` and `k = 3`, the result is `3 -> 2 -> 1 -> 6 -> 5 -> 4`.\n\nFor `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5` and `k = 3`, the result is `3 -> 2 -> 1 -> 4 -> 5` when incomplete groups remain unchanged.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nEach node is processed a constant number of times, giving O(n) time. The algorithm uses O(1) auxiliary space.\n\n**Common mistakes:**\n\nThe most common errors are losing the next group, incorrectly connecting the reversed groups, and reversing an incomplete final group when the problem says to leave it unchanged.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Identify each complete k-node group, reverse it in place, reconnect it to the previous group, and continue until fewer than k nodes remain.",
