    categorySlug: "data-structures-and-algorithms",
    subcategoryName: "Linked Lists",
    subcategorySlug: "linked-lists",
    experienceLevel: ExperienceLevel.FRESHER,
    difficulty: Difficulty.MEDIUM,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Common reported pattern: Palindrome-related string/linked-list problem. Source summary: recurring pattern in the supplied interview evidence bank; not a claim of one exact verbatim interview question.. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "This question works well in an interview because it forces the candidate to explain reason about linked lists in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code.",
    sampleAnswer: "For a linked list, find the middle with slow and fast pointers, reverse the second half, and compare the two halves node by node. Comparing values-not node addresses-is the important part. This gives O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space; restoring the second half afterward is a good production-minded touch.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe approach depends on whether the palindrome is represented as a string or a linked list. For a string, use two pointers from the beginning and end and compare characters while moving inward. For a singly linked list, use slow and fast pointers to locate the middle, reverse the second half, and compare the two halves.\n\n**String implementation:**\n\n```python\ndef is_palindrome(s):\n    left, right = 0, len(s) - 1\n\n    while left < right:\n        if s[left] != s[right]:\n            return False\n        left += 1\n        right -= 1\n\n    return True\n```\n\nThis takes O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space if the input is already a suitable string representation.\n\n**Linked-list implementation:**\n\n```python\ndef is_palindrome(head):\n    slow = fast = head\n\n    while fast and fast.next:\n        slow = slow.next\n        fast = fast.next.next\n\n    prev = None\n    while slow:\n        nxt = slow.next\n        slow.next = prev\n        prev = slow\n        slow = nxt\n\n    left, right = head, prev\n    while right:\n        if left.val != right.val:\n            return False\n        left = left.next\n        right = right.next\n\n    return True\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\n`1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 2 -> 1` is a palindrome because reading it forward and backward produces the same sequence.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nFor the linked-list approach, time is O(n) and auxiliary space is O(1). The list itself is modified temporarily because the second half is reversed. In production code, restore the second half if callers expect the original structure to remain unchanged.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> For strings, compare from both ends. For linked lists, find the midpoint, reverse the second half, compare both halves, and optionally restore the list.",
