    shortDescription: "Interview-ready explanation for: Solve linked-list coding questions with linear time complexity. Covers the core approach, edge cases, complexity, and a practical example.",
    explanation: "This is a useful interview question because it exposes the candidate's ability to reason about linked lists in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: GeeksforGeeks SDE interview experience. Source: GeeksforGeeks SDE Android Team interview experience. ==================================================== 2. LRU CACHE / HASHING ====================================================.",
    sampleAnswer: "The important idea is to avoid repeatedly walking the same nodes. For a linked-list problem, choose an invariant that lets each node be processed a constant number of times-for example, slow/fast pointers for a midpoint or cycle problem. That gives O(n) time instead of accidentally creating an O(n2) nested traversal. The exact algorithm still depends on the missing original problem statement.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nFor a linear-time linked-list problem, the goal is generally to process each node a constant number of times rather than repeatedly traversing the list. The main techniques are pointer manipulation, slow/fast pointers, dummy nodes, and maintaining auxiliary state when necessary.\n\nFor example, reversing a linked list requires only one pass:\n\n```python\ndef reverse(head):\n    prev = None\n    cur = head\n\n    while cur:\n        nxt = cur.next\n        cur.next = prev\n        prev = cur\n        cur = nxt\n\n    return prev\n```\n\nFor problems such as finding the middle node, use slow and fast pointers. For insertion or deletion near the head, a dummy node can simplify edge cases.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nA well-designed one-pass linked-list algorithm normally takes O(n) time. If only pointers are used, auxiliary space can be O(1).\n\n**Important considerations:**\n\nHandle an empty list, a single-node list, head deletion, tail deletion, and pointer reconnection carefully. Losing a reference to the remaining list is one of the most common linked-list mistakes.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Aim for one or a constant number of passes through the list, use pointer techniques instead of repeated traversal, and carefully preserve the next node before modifying links.",
