    explanation: "This question works well in an interview because it forces the candidate to explain recognize what is and is not specified before choosing an algorithm. For this strings item, the interview value comes from clarifying the missing constraint, stating the invariant, and then selecting a defensible approach rather than pretending an unspecified variant is known. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Microsoft SDE1. Source: LeetCode Microsoft SDE1 Noida interview experience.",
    sampleAnswer: "Two pointers are useful when the input has an order or when the answer can be maintained from both ends. Start by stating what each pointer represents and what invariant is preserved after moving one. The common goal is to turn a nested scan into a single O(n) pass. The exact reported problem should be clarified before committing to a specific implementation.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nTwo pointers are useful when two positions in an array or string can move through the data while maintaining a useful invariant. Common patterns include one pointer at each end, a slow/fast pair, and two pointers representing a variable-size window.\n\nFor a sorted array, an opposite-end approach can solve two-sum efficiently:\n\n```python\ndef two_sum_sorted(nums, target):\n    left, right = 0, len(nums) - 1\n\n    while left < right:\n        total = nums[left] + nums[right]\n\n        if total == target:\n            return [left, right]\n        elif total < target:\n            left += 1\n        else:\n            right -= 1\n\n    return []\n```\n\nIf the sum is too small, increasing the left pointer increases the sum because the array is sorted. If the sum is too large, decreasing the right pointer reduces it.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nFor an already sorted array, this takes O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.\n\n**Important:**\n\nThe correctness of this specific technique depends on the sorted-order property. If the array is unsorted, a hash map is typically more appropriate for Two Sum.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Use two pointers when the problem has an ordering or movement property that lets you eliminate impossible ranges without checking every pair.",
