detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nFor simple subsequence-style string matching, use one pointer for the pattern and one for the source string. Scan the source from left to right. Whenever the current source character matches the current pattern character, advance the pattern pointer.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef is_subsequence(pattern, text):\n    i = 0\n\n    for ch in text:\n        if i < len(pattern) and pattern[i] == ch:\n            i += 1\n\n    return i == len(pattern)\n```\n\nFor example, `abc` is a subsequence of `ahbgdc` because the characters appear in order, although they are not contiguous.\n\nFor exact substring matching, basic two pointers can lead to O(nm) worst-case time. For large-scale exact pattern matching, algorithms such as KMP can achieve O(n + m), while rolling-hash approaches can be useful in appropriate scenarios.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nThe subsequence check above is O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space, where n is the length of the source string.\n\n**Common mistake:**\n\nDo not confuse subsequence matching with substring matching. A substring must be contiguous; a subsequence does not.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Maintain a pointer into the pattern while scanning the source once. Advance the pattern pointer only when a matching character is found.",
