detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nTreat the blocker characters as boundaries that cannot belong to a valid path. Scan the string from left to right while maintaining the length of the current consecutive sequence of allowed lowercase characters. When a blocker is encountered, reset the current length to zero and update the maximum.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\ndef longest_safe_path(s):\n    current = 0\n    best = 0\n\n    for ch in s:\n        if 'a' <= ch <= 'z':\n            current += 1\n            best = max(best, current)\n        else:\n            current = 0\n\n    return best\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor `abc#defg##hi`, the longest uninterrupted lowercase-letter segment is `defg`, so the answer is 4.\n\nThe important point is to clarify what the blocker means. If blockers merely cannot be included but the path can somehow cross them, the problem may require a different interpretation. If blockers terminate a valid path, the linear scan above is sufficient.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nTime complexity is O(n), and auxiliary space is O(1).\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Maintain the length of the current valid lowercase segment, reset it whenever a blocker appears, and keep the maximum segment length seen so far.",
