    sampleAnswer: "The exact property is not included in the source, so the safe interview response is to clarify it before coding. For a traversal-based tree property, define what must be true at each node, choose DFS or BFS based on whether depth or level order matters, and maintain the smallest state needed to verify the property. Do not claim a specific company problem variant without the original statement.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nFirst translate the tree property into a condition that can be checked at each node or subtree. Then choose the traversal that naturally provides the information needed. DFS is commonly used for subtree properties, while BFS is useful when the property depends on levels or distances.\n\nFor example, to check whether a binary tree is a Sum Tree, perform postorder traversal. Each child must be evaluated before the parent because the parent's condition depends on subtree sums.\n\n```python\ndef check_sum_tree(root):\n    def dfs(node):\n        if node is None:\n            return True, 0\n\n        left_ok, left_sum = dfs(node.left)\n        right_ok, right_sum = dfs(node.right)\n\n        if not left_ok or not right_ok:\n            return False, 0\n\n        if node.left is None and node.right is None:\n            return True, node.val\n\n        valid = node.val == left_sum + right_sum\n        return valid, node.val + left_sum + right_sum\n\n    return dfs(root)[0]\n```\n\nThe exact traversal and state depend on the specified property. Examples include checking BST ordering, tree balance, symmetry, subtree sums, depth relationships, or level constraints.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nA properly designed traversal generally takes O(n) time because every node is processed once. Auxiliary space is O(h) for recursive DFS or potentially O(w) for BFS, where h is tree height and w is maximum width.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Define the local property and the information each subtree must return, then choose DFS or BFS so every node is processed once while carrying exactly the required state.",
