    sampleAnswer: "The source confirms a graph problem but not the exact statement. Start by deciding what the graph represents and whether edges are directed, weighted, or unweighted. Then choose BFS/DFS for reachability, Dijkstra for non-negative weighted shortest paths, topological sorting for dependency order, or union-find for connectivity under edge additions. State the representation and complexity before coding.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nFor a graph problem, first determine whether the graph is directed or undirected, weighted or unweighted, and whether the task asks for reachability, shortest path, connectivity, ordering, or optimization. Then choose the appropriate graph algorithm rather than applying BFS or DFS automatically.\n\nCommon choices include:\n\n- BFS for shortest paths in unweighted graphs and level-based exploration.\n- DFS for connectivity, cycle detection, components, and recursive graph exploration.\n- Dijkstra for non-negative weighted shortest paths.\n- Topological sort for dependency ordering in a DAG.\n- Union-Find for dynamic connectivity and component merging.\n- Bellman-Ford when negative edge weights may exist.\n\n**Example - BFS shortest path in an unweighted graph:**\n\n```python\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef shortest_path(graph, start, target):\n    queue = deque([(start, 0)])\n    visited = {start}\n\n    while queue:\n        node, distance = queue.popleft()\n\n        if node == target:\n            return distance\n\n        for neighbor in graph[node]:\n            if neighbor not in visited:\n                visited.add(neighbor)\n                queue.append((neighbor, distance + 1))\n\n    return -1\n```\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nBFS and DFS run in O(V + E) time with adjacency lists. Space complexity is O(V) for visited state and the traversal structure.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Classify the graph and the required operation first, then select BFS, DFS, Dijkstra, topological sorting, Union-Find, or another graph algorithm based on those properties.",
