    sampleAnswer: "A greedy algorithm commits to a locally best choice, but the interview-worthy part is explaining why that choice is safe. Look for an exchange argument, a cut property, or another proof that an optimal solution can be transformed to include the greedy choice. If you cannot justify the choice, consider dynamic programming or another method instead.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA greedy algorithm makes the best locally optimal choice at each step with the expectation that these choices produce a globally optimal solution. The critical part of a greedy solution is not the implementation but proving that the local choice is safe.\n\nTypical greedy problems include interval scheduling, minimum number of platforms or resources, activity selection, fractional knapsack, and some jump or coverage problems.\n\n**Example - Maximum Number of Non-Overlapping Intervals:**\n\nSort intervals by their ending time and repeatedly choose the interval that finishes earliest while remaining compatible with the previously selected interval.\n\n```python\ndef max_non_overlapping(intervals):\n    intervals.sort(key=lambda x: x[1])\n    count = 0\n    last_end = float('-inf')\n\n    for start, end in intervals:\n        if start >= last_end:\n            count += 1\n            last_end = end\n\n    return count\n```\n\nChoosing the earliest finishing interval leaves the maximum possible remaining space for future intervals.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nSorting costs O(n log n), and the scan costs O(n), giving O(n log n) total time. The additional algorithmic space is O(1) apart from the sorting implementation.\n\n**Common mistake:**\n\nDo not assume that every problem with an obvious local choice is greedy. Compare against dynamic programming or other approaches and establish why the greedy choice cannot hurt the optimum.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Identify the local choice, prove that it is safe, sort or structure the input to make that choice efficient, and then process each item once.",
