    sampleAnswer: "Because the source does not give the exact variant, first clarify whether the problem means a contiguous subarray, a subsequence with no adjacent picks, or a path through a structure. For a non-adjacent maximum subsequence, keep two states: best up to the previous element, and best when the current element is taken. The transition is max(skip, take). This is O(n) time and O(1) space.",
detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe phrase maximum-sum subsequence can represent several variants, so the first step is to clarify the constraints. If arbitrary elements may be selected while preserving order, and there is no restriction on adjacent selections, the problem is simply to sum positive values when the empty subsequence is allowed. More interesting variants impose constraints such as no two adjacent elements, fixed selection count, or distance restrictions.\n\n**Example - No Two Adjacent Elements:**\n\n```python\ndef max_sum_subsequence(nums):\n    prev2 = 0\n    prev1 = 0\n\n    for x in nums:\n        current = max(prev1, prev2 + x)\n        prev2 = prev1\n        prev1 = current\n\n    return prev1\n```\n\nHere `prev1` represents the best result up to the previous element and `prev2` represents the best result before that.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nTime is O(n) and auxiliary space is O(1).\n\n**Important distinction:**\n\nA subsequence is not required to be contiguous. If the problem instead asks for a contiguous range, it becomes a subarray problem and techniques such as Kadane's algorithm may apply.\n\nIf exactly k elements must be selected, the DP state usually needs another dimension representing how many elements have been selected.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Clarify the subsequence constraints first. Define a DP state around position and any additional restriction, derive the recurrence, and optimize the state only after correctness is established.",
