detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nBinary-tree problems involving traversal, views, and paths usually require choosing the traversal based on what information the problem needs.\n\nFor standard traversals:\n\n- Preorder: root, left, right.\n- Inorder: left, root, right.\n- Postorder: left, right, root.\n- Level order: breadth-first traversal by depth.\n\nFor top and bottom views, associate each node with a horizontal distance from the root. BFS is useful because nodes are processed level by level. For top view, keep the first node encountered at each horizontal distance. For bottom view, overwrite the stored node whenever another node at that horizontal distance is encountered later.\n\nFor path problems such as maximum path sum, DFS is often appropriate because subtree results can be combined at each node.\n\n**Example - maximum path sum:**\n\n```python\ndef max_path_sum(root):\n    answer = float('-inf')\n\n    def dfs(node):\n        nonlocal answer\n        if node is None:\n            return 0\n\n        left = max(0, dfs(node.left))\n        right = max(0, dfs(node.right))\n\n        answer = max(answer, node.val + left + right)\n        return node.val + max(left, right)\n\n    dfs(root)\n    return answer\n```\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nMost traversal-based solutions process each node once, giving O(n) time. Auxiliary space is O(h) for recursive DFS or O(w) for BFS, depending on the traversal.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Match the traversal to the required information: DFS for subtree and path properties, BFS for level-based problems, and horizontal distance plus BFS for common tree-view problems.",
