detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nGraph problems often appear indirectly as networks, dependencies, routes, transformations, relationships, or state transitions. The first step is to identify the vertices and edges and determine whether edges are directed, weighted, or unweighted.\n\nThen select the algorithm according to the requirement:\n\n- BFS for unweighted shortest paths and level exploration.\n- DFS for reachability, connected components, and many cycle-detection problems.\n- Dijkstra for non-negative weighted shortest paths.\n- Topological sort for dependency ordering in a DAG.\n- Union-Find for connectivity and component merging.\n- 0-1 BFS when edge weights are only 0 and 1.\n\n**Example - cycle detection in a directed graph:**\n\n```python\ndef has_cycle(graph):\n    state = [0] * len(graph)\n\n    def dfs(node):\n        if state[node] == 1:\n            return True\n        if state[node] == 2:\n            return False\n\n        state[node] = 1\n        for nei in graph[node]:\n            if dfs(nei):\n                return True\n\n        state[node] = 2\n        return False\n\n    return any(state[node] == 0 and dfs(node)\n               for node in range(len(graph)))\n```\n\nHere state `1` means the node is currently in the recursion stack, while state `2` means it has been completely processed. Encountering a node in state `1` indicates a directed cycle.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nWith an adjacency-list representation, DFS-based graph algorithms commonly run in O(V + E) time and O(V) auxiliary traversal space.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Recognize the hidden graph structure, classify the graph and objective, then select the algorithm whose invariant directly matches the required operation.",
