detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nHeap-based problems are appropriate when the algorithm repeatedly needs access to the current minimum or maximum while elements are being inserted or removed. A heap provides O(1) access to its root and O(log n) insertion and removal.\n\nFor top-k problems, it is often better to maintain a heap of size k rather than sorting all n elements.\n\n**Example - Top K Frequent Elements:**\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nimport heapq\n\ndef top_k_frequent(nums, k):\n    freq = Counter(nums)\n    heap = []\n\n    for value, count in freq.items():\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (count, value))\n\n        if len(heap) > k:\n            heapq.heappop(heap)\n\n    return [value for count, value in heap]\n```\n\nThe min-heap keeps the k elements with the highest frequencies. When a new candidate has a frequency greater than the smallest frequency currently retained, the smallest candidate is removed.\n\n**Common heap patterns:**\n\nA min-heap is useful for retaining the largest k elements. A max-heap is useful for retaining the smallest k elements. Two heaps can maintain a partition, such as the lower and upper halves of a stream for median calculation.\n\n**Complexity:**\n\nFor the top-k frequency example, frequency counting takes O(n), and maintaining a heap of at most k distinct values costs O(m log k), where m is the number of distinct values. Overall this is O(n + m log k) time and O(m + k) space.\n\n**Common mistake:**\n\nDo not automatically sort when only an extreme or top-k result is needed. A heap can reduce the cost from O(n log n) to O(n log k) when k is much smaller than n.\n\n**Interview-ready summary:**\n\n> Use a heap when repeated min/max selection is required, and maintain only the necessary candidates when the problem asks for top-k or kth-element information.",
